  "notes": [
    {
      "topic": "admission",
      "text": "Students who have studied French previously are encouraged to take the French Placement Exam offered through the Language Learning Research Center. High school language study is not counted for college credit. Students should consult the FRN Program Coordinator if they believe they should be placed in a different level course."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "FRN 475/FRN 476 (Undergraduate Teaching Practicum) may not count for the major or minor."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "Concentration A provides preparation for graduate study in literature. Both concentrations provide background for law, government, international relations, business, banking, hotel management, or translation and interpretation. Students interested in teaching French in secondary schools may choose either concentration, but must have 36 credits in FRN before the State of New York will certify them. Foreign language education students are also highly encouraged to study more than one foreign language."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "All students should consult with the Director of the French Program. Transfer students must take at least 18 credits of French in residence at Stony Brook; this applies to transfer students only."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "Optional honors program (not modeled): majors with a cumulative GPA of 3.00 and an average of 3.50 in French through the junior year may write a senior thesis by enrolling in FRN 495. Deadlines: April 30 for fall, November 30 for spring. Consult the Director of Undergraduate Studies."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "The Foreign Language Secondary Teacher Education Program is a separate program; see the Education and Teacher Certification entry (not modeled)."
    },
    {
      "topic": "sbc",
      "text": "Students completing the Stony Brook Curriculum must complete a course satisfying the WRTD learning objective. The Upper Division Writing Requirement is consistent in most cases with the SBC learning outcomes for WRTD."
    }
  ],
